}README.md:10:| Intended intuition | invariant — recognize a circulant coordinate change and its finite geometric inverse |
README.md:13:This generator turns Zanella and Zullo, [*Vertex properties of maximum scattered linear sets of \(PG(1,q^n)\)*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.05611), into an exact witness problem.  The solver receives a codimension-two projection vertex \(\Gamma\subset PG(d-1,q^d)\), its Frobenius collineation, and two homogeneous equations.  It must give the unique projective point whose first \(d-3\) Frobenius conjugates form the long independent chain in \(\Gamma\).  Checking the point needs only substitutions, cyclic shifts, and row rank modulo the prime \(q\).
README.md:19:An efficient algorithm exists and is not hidden: stack the \(2(d-3)\) shifted vertex equations and compute their one-dimensional nullspace.  It costs \(O(d^3)\) exact field operations and, at shipping \(d=97\), averaged 24,678 counted operations and 0.021 seconds over eight seeds.  The constructed coordinate change is \(B=I+aS^r\).  Seeing that invariant replaces elimination with the finite geometric identity for \(B^{-1}\), at most 204 modular operations.  Generation carries the known point through that identity; it never solves a generated instance.
README.md:50:| hard | 15 | 97 | 32771 | provisional shipping preset; local gates pass |
README.md:59:| G4 | 0/200,000 hits from uniform normalized points of \(PG(96,32771)\) |
README.md:61:| G6 | four attacks each 0/8; reference elimination 8/8 as expected, mean 24,678 operations |
README.md:64:| G9(c) | worst of 1,000 shipping seeds: 576 characters / 144 estimated tokens / 97 atoms; 204 intended operations |
README.md:96:- This family is deliberately easy for a computer with modular elimination; its claim is only Track B.  The 0.021-second mean reference time is evidence of that honesty, not Track-A hardness.
README.md:97:- The generated vertices have equations over the distinguished base field and one binomial circulant disguise.  They do not sample arbitrary vertices over \(GF(q^d)\).
README.md:99:- The panel tests coordinate scores, a sparse-nullspace greedy choice, 256 random restarts, and constant/axis ansatzes.  It does not test every possible circulant-factorization heuristic; recognizing the circulant is the intended route.
README.md:100:- `canonical_key` removes generated circulant/cyclic disguises and keys on the LP norm up to inversion.  Additional projective equivalences outside those tested could make it over-distinguish; full projective equivalence classification is not attempted.
